INT. CLOCK TOWER MECHANISM ROOM - NIGHT

Iron gears the size of cartwheels turn behind a dusty rail. Moonlight through the louvres.

JUNE KOWALCZYK, thirties, a clockmaker in leather gloves, crouches by the main wheel with a lantern while REGGIE FAIRWEATHER, fifties, the town clerk, climbs the last rung of the ladder, wheezing, and drops a folded paper on the floor that June snatches before he can reach it, reads, crumples, and shoves into her pocket as Reggie lunges for her wrist.

JUNE
Reggie, the hands stopped at 11:42 on the night your brother died.

REGGIE
June, it's a clock.

JUNE
Somebody held them there. With a pin through the escapement.

Reggie backs toward the ladder, hand on the rail, one boot already finding the rung. June blocks the way, lantern raised, her shadow swelling across the gears.

REGGIE
You'll fall.

JUNE
Not me.

The main wheel clunks. The room shudders. Both of them look up as the great bell above them begins, slowly, to ring.
